  "notes": "Northern (Hanoi) Vietnamese in chữ quốc ngữ, following Kirby (2011), the IPA Illustration for this variety. The mapping is stated over whole rimes rather than single letters, because the rime is the unit quốc ngữ makes predictable: ⟨anh ach⟩ are [ɛŋ ɛk] and ⟨inh ich ênh êch⟩ are [iŋ ik eŋ ek] (velar fronting, phonetically pre-velar [ŋ̟ k̟] — a narrow detail this spec does not write); ⟨ong oc ông ôc ung uc⟩ take the doubly articulated finals [ŋ͡m k͡p] after the rounded vowels /u o ɔ/; ⟨ay au ây âu⟩ spell the short vowels /ă ɤ̆/; and ⟨iê/ia⟩, ⟨uô/ua⟩, ⟨ươ/ưa⟩ are the three falling diphthongs /iə uə ɯə/, whose spelling alternates with the presence of a coda. Onsets follow the same source: ⟨tr⟩ and ⟨ch⟩ are merged as /tɕ/ and ⟨s⟩ and ⟨x⟩ as /s/ in modern Hanoi speech, ⟨d gi r⟩ are all /z/, ⟨g gh⟩ is the fricative /ɣ/, and ⟨b đ⟩ are the implosives /ɓ ɗ/. Southern Vietnamese keeps several of these distinctions and is not described here. ⟨eng ec⟩ and ⟨oong ooc⟩ are the long /ɛː ɔː/ rimes: quốc ngữ leaves those spellings for the long vowels because ⟨anh ach⟩ and ⟨ong oc⟩ are taken by the fronted and the labial-velar rimes, and Kirby (p. 384) sets the pairs out as [sɛːŋ] xẻng against [sɛŋ] xanh and [sɔːŋ] xoong against [sɔŋ͡m] xong. The length is written; the accompanying coda-place difference is the same narrow detail the rest of this spec leaves out. ⟨ec ooc⟩ are the checked parallels of ⟨eng oong⟩ — Kirby's pairs are both sonorant-final, so the checked members carry the same vowel by the orthographic generalisation rather than by his example. All six tones are written in the orthography, so tone is derivable and is emitted, in Chao letters at the end of the syllable. Kirby's eight-tone analysis is followed: six tones on open or sonorant-final syllables, and only the sắc-spelled (D1) and nặng-spelled (D2) tones on syllables closed by an oral stop, which is why the checked rimes carry two readings and every other rime carries six. The Chao letters are a notation for the tone categories, chosen to match the ipa-dict vi_N convention; the categories themselves are what the orthography decides. The grapheme and allophone tables are generated by scripts/gen_vietnamese.py. The vox_communis benchmark row for Vietnamese is a notation-agreement artifact, not a verdict on this spec: its gold merges the contrastive tones ngang and huyền under one tone letter on 39.1% of its words, and folding the remaining transcription conventions out of both sides takes its PER from 0.5596 to 0.0246 (scripts/fold_vi_notation.py). See docs/languages/vi.md.",
